Commit 3c2c53d5a0:

fix(ai): source scope target ranges from the compiled commands (#3061)

### 🎯 What does this PR do?
Since the workflow-scope centralization, two prompt capabilities could
never confirm: the application derived scope target ranges twice — once
per-capability by hand in getApplicationWorkflowScope, once from the
compiled command batch — and where the hand-mirror disagreed
(syncopated-arpeggio returned no ranges, bass-processing-copy derived
from its target region), planAgentRun rejected every run as a provider
scope violation. The application was arguing with itself. Target ranges
now have one source: the batch compiler's range derivation is extracted
into a Command use case, a new AiRuntime use case measures planned
actions by compiling them through the same migration path with cloned
inputs and no handler side effects, and
composeVerifiedProviderProposalScope uses those ranges on both its
branches. Strategies keep what they legitimately own — target ids and
protection widening — and can no longer state ranges at all, so the
disagreement is unrepresentable. The planAgentRun tripwire stays: it
still catches parse-time and compile-time divergence, which is what
exposed this bug. An audit of all six strategies found three agreed with
the compiler only by luck; all are single-sourced now. A second latent
defect fell out: bass-processing-copy claimed its context section as a
mutated target, which would have rejected the capability on target ids
right after the range fix; the section is context, not a target. Two
unrelated pre-existing reds found during verification are filed, not
touched.

