* fix(dsp): report Bacteria's real oversampling latency to the host

BacteriaEngine::latency_samples returned a hardcoded 0. Before the
oversampler rewrite that was a small lie -- the broken 2-tap filter had
~0-1 sample of delay. It is now a measurable one: 2x/4x/8x introduce
6.5/9.75/11.375 base-rate samples, and Wave 3 wired reported device
latency into PDC, so the host silently miscompensated by up to ~11
samples whenever oversampling was on. The rewrite converted a cosmetic
wrong into a real one, so it belongs in this lane.

Oversampling is a PER-BAND parameter (bandN_oversampling) and bands are
summed, so the device reports the longest ACTIVE band. All six
BandChains are allocated up front while only band_count run, so the
report takes band_count -- a stale factor on an inactive band must not
inflate it.

Reported from the configured factor, not from the live
distortion_enabled/enabled/mute flags. Those toggle during performance
and renegotiating PDC on every toggle is worse than a stable worst case;
over-reporting is the safe direction, under-reporting slides the track
early against everything else.

NOT folded in: StftProcessor's windowing latency on the spectral path.
That omission predates this lane and StftProcessor exposes no accessor
for it, so a guessed number would be worse than a stated omission. It
needs its own finding.

Red-first: both tests fail against the restored hardcoded 0
(reported_latency_tracks_the_oversampling_factor expects 0/7/10/11,
reported_latency_follows_the_longest_active_band expects 11 then 7) and
pass on the real computation.

