Commit 2d23cbcbd1:

fix(transport): keep the scheduler on the audio clock after a main-thread stall (#6004)

### 🎯 What does this PR do?
When the browser's main thread stalled for longer than the 0.1 s
look-ahead during playback (garbage collection, a heavy render, a long
task), the scheduler advanced the song position by only 0.1 s while the
audio clock kept running. Everything scheduled afterwards (the click,
MIDI notes, newly started clips, automation) then played late against
long audio clips and frozen tracks that were already sounding, by the
stall minus 0.1 s, and the offset accumulated with every stall until the
next loop wrap or stop. The clamp's justification, that a resumed
AudioContext leaps its clock, contradicts the Web Audio spec, where a
suspended context's time does not advance. The song position now follows
the audio clock across a stall. Events that fell due during the stall
are dropped rather than replayed as a burst, sustained audio rejoins
mid-buffer, stored controllers and channel bend are chased to their
value at the playhead, and a stall crossing several loop passes lands on
the pass the audio clock is in. Gestures captured right after a stall
are dated on the same clock.

### 🧪 How to test
1. Play a project with a long audio stem, a MIDI drum track and the
metronome on. In Chrome DevTools, Performance panel, set CPU throttling
to 20x for a few seconds mid-playback, then turn it off. The drums and
click stay locked to the stem afterwards, with no flam and no burst of
late hits.
2. Place an audio clip that starts inside the span a stall covers. When
playback reaches it after the stall, it enters part-way through, in time
with the stem, instead of starting late from its head.
3. Loop a region on a Grand Boule track with a sustain pedal pressed and
then lifted later in the loop. Stall the main thread across the lift.
The pedal is up afterwards and no notes hang.

### 📌 Related issues & additional notes
None.

