Commit 2b67adcd72 (diff trimmed to the part I care about):

feat(electron): carry the native IPC surface in the Electron shell

Adds the preload bridge, the command router, the event path, plugin
scanning in its own process, and the quit cascade (REQ-004, REQ-006,
REQ-007, REQ-012). Nothing in `src/` or `src-tauri/src/` changes: the
renderer still runs its browser path, so this is observable through its
own specs and the dev shell and inert for the product until the seam
moves.

The exposed surface is derived, not copied. The Tauri capability is the
product's decision about what a renderer may invoke, so the 60 exposed
commands are exactly what it grants and the 17 denied ones are exactly
the registered commands it withholds. `commands.spec.ts` re-derives all
three sets from Rust at test time.

The router is opaque: it forwards the renderer's positional argument
array to the addon and never reads inside it, leaving validation at the
napi-rs boundary where serde had it. Its two transport adaptations —
`Uint8Array` to `Buffer`, and a stream id appended as a trailing
emitter — read no field.

Pushed events are fire-and-forget and drop when there is no window;
progress coalesces to latest per correlation so a lagging producer
cannot queue. A command stream is the opposite: its events are queued
when undeliverable, that queue is capped, and reaching the cap fails the
request, because a response body missing a chunk decodes as if it were
complete.

Scanning runs in a `utilityProcess`, so a hostile plugin takes down a
process the app can rebuild rather than the one holding the audio
device. The Rust policy could not re-enter that worker by re-executing
`current_exe()` — under Electron that is a runtime handed a script, not
the application — so it now reads a declared launch command from the
environment, and its argument parse locates the worker marker at any
index rather than a fixed one. Every scan-policy invariant is unchanged.

The preload is bundled to one CommonJS file. Electron gives a sandboxed
preload a `require` polyfill that resolves only `electron`, `events`,
`timers` and `url`, so a relative import of a sibling would throw inside
the sandbox and leave `window.sourdaw` undefined with nothing logged.

