Commit 4c5e19dd6f:

fix(electron): bound the crash recovery and tighten the asset handler

Six review findings on the shell scaffold, all in the same class: code that
looked correct because nothing had made it fail yet.

- `render-process-gone` recreated the window without limit. A crash that
  reproduces on load — bad build, GPU fault, a project restored at startup —
  turned that into a spin the user could not read a failure out of. It now
  recreates at most three times a minute and then stops with an error dialog.
  It also only acts on the session window; a crashed webview owned by something
  else no longer resurrects itself as the main window.
- The window-open handler parsed an untrusted URL outside a try/catch, so an
  unparseable target threw into Electron's window-open path instead of being
  denied. It now denies and logs.
- `--enable-features=WebAudioConfigurableRenderQuantum` is gone. Nothing in the
  renderer passes `renderSizeHint`, so the switch changed no behaviour, and the
  Chromium implementation carries an open renderer-memory-safety bug
  (crbug 485292589). Turning on an unused feature that widens the renderer's
  attack surface is a pure cost.
- `existsSync` matched directories, so a route colliding with `dist/assets` or
  `dist/wasm` reached `net.fetch` over `file://` and answered 200 with a
  generated directory listing. The check is now `statSync(...).isFile()`, with
  a second guard at the fetch site that also covers `/samples/**`.
- The host check used `url.hostname`, which ignores a port: `app://sourdaw:1/`
  would have been served as the app origin while Chromium treats it as a
  different one. It compares `url.host` now.
- The comment over `net.fetch` claimed Range support. Electron drops request
  headers on a `file://` fetch, so it has none. The comment says what the code
  does and what that costs.

The isolation probe grows the assertion that AC-018 actually rests on. A 200 is
not enough for either asset class: `WebAssembly` streaming refuses anything not
typed `application/wasm`, and `audioWorklet.addModule` refuses a non-JavaScript
MIME, so a handler serving both as `text/plain` would pass the old probe and
still leave the DAW with no DSP. The probe now records both Content-Types and
performs a real `compileStreaming` and a real `addModule`.

